background:
The US death-row population has declined for 20 consecutive years, with around 2,100 [prisoners currently facing execution](https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/death-row/overview). However, capital punishment remains in the headlines, in part due to [Florida's increasing numbers of executions](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/30/magazine/florida-death-penalty.html). In mid August, [Florida executed its 13th death row prisoner of the year](https://apnews.com/article/florida-execution-william-silvia-9ece9e5bba0484cdd83e53fd0918145a). Texas remains the state with the most executions since the reinstatement of the death penalty in the 1970s.

Not all executions that are planned go ahead [on schedule](https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/executions/upcoming-executions/outcomes-of-warrants/outcomes-of-death-warrants-in-2026), either due to reprieves, appeals, executive orders, or in the case of [Christa Pike in Tennessee](https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/tag/christa-pike), a failed execution. A review was called for by the Governor and [the Department of Correction Commissioner resigned](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/03/us/tennessee-prison-commissioner-resigns-christa-pike.html). However, [the 30th person executed of the year](https://www.local10.com/news/2026/10/07/texas-carries-out-nations-first-execution-since-christa-pikes-failed-lethal-injection/) occurred a week later in Texas.&#x20;

[Nine more executions are scheduled](https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/executions/upcoming-executions) to take place between October 13 and December 31, 2026. If they all go ahead, the total for the nation in 2026 would be 39.
